<h2>The prosecution case</h2>
<p>The appellant drove the school van in which the child, then about ten years old and in Class V, travelled. On 14 February 2018, the prosecution alleged, he made her touch his penis on the pretext of teaching her to change gears, and threatened to kill her if she told anyone. She told her mother the same day, and the FIR was registered. The trial court convicted him under Section 10 of the POCSO Act (he was acquitted of the charge under Section 506 IPC) and imposed seven years&rsquo; rigorous imprisonment with fine and compensation.</p>

<h2>The three pillars of the affirmance</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Age, formally proved.</strong> The admission form, the school&rsquo;s admission register and the MCD birth certificate established the date of birth (13 January 2008), following the approach in <em>Ashwani Kumar Saxena</em>. Age is the jurisdictional fact of every POCSO case, and here it was documentary and unchallenged.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. The child&rsquo;s testimony.</strong> Applying <em>State v. Pankaj Chaudhary</em> and <em>Ganesan v. State</em>, the court held that &ldquo;the testimony of the victim, if found to be cogent, credible and trustworthy, can form the sole basis of conviction&rdquo;. Her account was consistent on all material particulars and withstood cross-examination; the mother&rsquo;s evidence of immediate disclosure corroborated it.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. The unrebutted presumption.</strong> With the foundational facts in place, Section 29 POCSO presumed the commission of the offence. The defence — false implication arising from a van-fare dispute — was supported by no evidence and squarely denied by the mother. The presumption therefore operated with full force.</div>
</div>

<h2>The relationship of trust</h2>
<p>The judgment&rsquo;s most quotable line addresses the setting rather than the elements: &ldquo;A school van driver is entrusted with the safety and well-being of children&rdquo;, the court observed, likening the role to that of a parent. The observation matters for sentencing. Within the five-to-seven-year band of Section 10, the breach of a custodial relationship of trust justified the maximum, and the High Court declined to soften it. The compensation of Rs 3,00,000 under Section 33(8) of the Act read with Rule 9 of the POCSO Rules, 2020 was likewise maintained, as was the set-off under Section 428 CrPC.</p>

<h2>What the decision reinforces</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Sole testimony doctrine.</strong> POCSO trials frequently turn on the child alone. The settled test is quality, not quantity: cogency, consistency and credibility, assessed with sensitivity to the child&rsquo;s age.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Immediate disclosure.</strong> Same-day disclosure to a parent remains among the strongest corroborative circumstances, closing the space for theories of tutoring or afterthought.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Defences need evidence.</strong> A motive theory — a fare dispute, a quarrel, a grudge — must be put to witnesses and proved. An unsubstantiated suggestion leaves Section 29 untouched.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Surrender follows.</strong> Where sentence was suspended pending appeal, dismissal carries a surrender direction — here, within four weeks.</div>
</div>

<h2>A note on the current law</h2>
<p>The offence predates 1 July 2024, so the IPC and CrPC applied alongside the POCSO Act. For later offences, the procedural frame is the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023, and the general penal provisions are found in the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 — but the POCSO Act itself continues unchanged as the special statute, and with it the presumption under Section 29, the child-friendly trial procedure, and the compensation regime under the 2020 Rules.</p>
